            <div xml:id="d326">

                <pb facs="0409" n="395" sameAs="frus:frus1944v06/pg395"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">800.91293/21: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Gauss</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Chungking</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1944-04-07T14:00:00+07:00">April 7, 1944—2
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received April 9—9:30 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>622. Reliably informed that British correspondent who threatened to expose
                    Votaw’s background if latter should represent AP on Yenan trip (Embassy’s 556,
                    March 30 and Department’s 421, April 1, 10 a.m. [<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >p.m.</hi>] expressed to Moosa, local AP correspondent, strong criticism of
                    such choice and that Moosa has now canceled Votaw’s appointment as AP
                    representative on trip. Votaw will probably make trip as representative of
                    “other organizations”.</p>
                <p>Foregoing confirmed indirectly by Moosa who has informed officer of Embassy
                    [that] Yenan trip too long for regular AP staff member but that while Votaw only
                    available person to represent AP his connection with Information Ministry makes
                    him unsuitable.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Gauss</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
